From 3cda9587c2529e0631a3bb13ef60fcb925e00285 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 10:46:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: more kinds of AP, DomD's link state, and SELinux
 enforcing

DomA's Wi-Fi is a relay that mirrors DomD's scan as hostapd shadow BSSes
on mac80211_hwsim, the shadow AP's start-up, and DomaWifiRelay, the app
that hands a typed passphrase to the relay. The scripts, the app, the
overlay and the policy are the same as the Raspberry Pi 5 product's in
the AGL layer (device/agl/xenvm-trout-rpi5, device/agl/doma-common).

More kinds of AP, and a lighter relay:
- Open APs get an open shadow BSS (wpa=0), listed without a lock; the
  relay notices its station joining one and forwards a connect with an
  empty passphrase. A row switches between open and secured only once
  three publications agree. SAE APs keep WPA2 shadow BSSes (DomD's
  agent chooses SAE for the real AP).
- Up to 20 APs (wlan2_1 .. wlan2_19, within hostapd's BSSID mask rule),
  ordered by the publication, the station's, the seeded and the missing
  ones. No sample AP in the seed: with no usable row both generators
  emit one hidden idle BSS so hostapd still starts.
- The newest 64 typed passphrases are kept in learned.conf (wifi, 0600,
  never logged), and only once DomD has joined with one.
- Shell builtins replace about 42 forks a publication (relay CPU 9% ->
  0.25% on this board with the relay of 2026-10-11), a row a
  publication omits is kept until three omit it, and an identical
  hostapd.conf restarts nothing.
- The product sets persist.vendor.doma.wifi_relay_bounce_on_aps=false
  with ...bounce_min_s=300: a changed list restarts hostapd at most
  once every 300 s and only while no station is associated (the
  restarts took 17% of 200% of DomA's CPU during playback on this
  board, 2026-10-11; the 300 s limit itself was not measured).
- ssid_hostapd_safe() and psk_valid(), in the relay and in
  doma-shadow-ap.sh, forked printf | wc -c for every SSID and PSK of a
  publication: about 1 s of DomA's CPU per publication (45 s: printf
  38, wc 38, fork 126), enough to stall video playing in a WebView on
  this board (2026-10-26). They now count with byte_len(), mksh's ${#s}
  with its UTF-8 mode turned off for the count; on DomA's mksh (R59) the
  old and new checks agreed on 72 cases in both modes, and the relay
  ran 45 s with printf and wc 0 and fork 43.

DomD's link state:
- Every connect has an id ("<pid>-<n>") that DomD's answer carries
  back; PENDING keeps the unanswered ones (300 s).
- LOST for the station's SSID takes its row out and restarts hostapd,
  and holds the SSID until DomD reports it BACK, it leaves the scan and
  returns, or 300 s pass. FAILED with WRONG_KEY puts the row's PSK back
  and drops the passphrase if sent again within 300 s; another FAILED
  takes the row out for 300 s while the station is on it.

SELinux enforcing (the product still boots permissive):
- The shadow AP, the relay and hostapd run as uid wifi; the oneshot and
  hostapd with NET_ADMIN and NET_RAW, the relay with none. The oneshot
  is vendor.doma_shadow_ap, so the relay may read its status.
- The scripts use only /vendor's tools (toybox_vendor and awk, and
  iw_vendor, now installed) and cuttlefish's apexes (rename_netiface),
  on #!/vendor/bin/sh. hostapd is checked by its init status, the radios
  by the shadow AP's netdev. A virt_wifi wlan0 left by a fallback this
  boot is no longer deleted (nothing in /vendor can): the oneshot asks
  for a reboot; the fallback's setup_wifi runs as uid wifi with
  NET_ADMIN.
- doma_shadow_ap.te is rewritten from what the scripts do; the drop-box
  has a type of its own (doma_wifi_req_file) that system_app.te gives
  the app. init relabels it and hands the files written as root to wifi.
- DomaWifiRelay follows the relay's init status (a core domain may not
  read the vendor property), runs for the system user only, does the
  Wi-Fi cycle the oneshot used to do with settings and cmd (off, wait
  for off, on, if Wi-Fi is saved on), and makes what it writes 0640.

No internet before DomD joined: AAOS can check a network before DomD
has the real one and disable it as NO_INTERNET_PERMANENT. The relay
writes DomD's reports into req/relay.state, and the app enables such a
network again while DomD has it, once per report. DomaWifiOverlay, a
product RRO, makes the disable last 10 minutes instead of for good.

Tested on a PC: the relay under mksh (186 checks in five suites), the
app against the system stubs, and the AOSP build of selinux_policy (with
the neverallow check), DomaWifiRelay, DomaWifiOverlay and iw_vendor for
this product, on a tree that differs from this one only in comments. On
this board, with the relay of 2026-10-11 (before the ids, LOST and the
SELinux changes): the fork reduction and the hysteresis; and on
2026-10-27, with this commit's relay and doma-shadow-ap put into DomA's
vendor image, beside an init.xenvm-hwsim.rc that also had the threaded
NAPI lines of a later commit (this commit's rc had run on the board
before): the bring-up completed (the shadow AP reported done), DomA
joined DomD's AP and pinged, and the relay ran 45 s with no printf or wc
fork. On the Raspberry Pi 5, with that product's copies of these files,
which differ from this commit's only in comments, over an adb remount:
the services, labels and Wi-Fi cycle, restarts with no denial also with
setenforce 1, a typed passphrase for a new AP repaired and joined
(permissive and setenforce 1), and the no-internet repair for an online
line written as the relay writes it. LOST was seen there with an earlier
relay; BACK has run on a PC only. Not yet run on this board: the open-AP
path, the idle BSS, learned.conf, 20 shadow BSSes, the rate limit, LOST
and BACK, the app's changes, the overlay, setenforce 1 and the
no-internet repair.

